\description{
Estimate the false discovery proportion among a search's discoveries from its entrapment hits, as a lower bound, a combined upper bound and a paired upper bound, at each of several q-value thresholds.

An entrapment experiment adds peptides that cannot be in the sample (from another species, or
made from the target peptides) to the search database. Every entrapment the search reports is
then a known false discovery, which lets you check the search's FDR claim instead of trusting it.
mzLib's \code{EntrapmentFdp.Sweep} computes the three valid estimators of Wen et al. 2025 (the method
FDRBench implements) at each q-value threshold:
}
\details{
- \code{lower_bound} = N_E / (N_T + N_E). Above the FDR you claimed, the search \strong{fails} to control
  it. Below, it proves nothing.
- \code{combined} = N_E (1 + 1/r) / (N_T + N_E). An upper bound: at or below the claimed FDR, it is
  \strong{evidence of control}.
- \code{paired}: a tighter upper bound when each target has exactly one entrapment made from it
  (\code{ratio = 1}), using whether each discovered entrapment's own target was discovered too.

The "sample" estimator N_E / (r N_T) is invalid in both directions and is deliberately absent.
}
\value{
An \code{mzlibr_entrapment_fdp_estimates}:

  - \code{records}, a data.frame with one row per q-value threshold with a discovery:
    \code{q_value_threshold} (a q-value, fraction), where a candidate is discovered at or below it;
    \code{target_count}, N_T, targets discovered (discoveries); \code{entrapment_count}, N_E, entrapments
    discovered (discoveries); and three estimates of the false discovery proportion:
    \code{lower_bound} (fraction of discoveries), a \strong{lower bound} (above your claimed FDR, the
